        "input": "2\n5 2\n1 2 4 3 2\n1 1 3 5 5\n2 1 2 3 5\n6 2\n3 5 6 13 12 20\n1 1 4 5 6\n3 2 3 4 6",
        "output": "2\n2\n4\n4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The array is A=[1,2,4,3,2]A = [1, 2, 4, 3, 2]A=[1,2,4,3,2].\n\nQuery 111: the ranges are [1,3][1, 3][1,3] and [5,5][5, 5][5,5], and k=1k = 1k=1. There are three pairs of (i,j)(i, j)(i,j): (1,5),(2,5),(3,5)(1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 5)(1,5),(2,5),(3,5).\n\nA1⊕A5=1⊕2=3=112A_1 \\oplus A_5 = 1 \\oplus 2 = 3 = 11_2A1​⊕A5​=1⊕2=3=112​ has its first bit set\nA2⊕A5=2⊕2=0=02A_2 \\oplus A_5 = 2 \\oplus 2 = 0 = 0_2A2​⊕A5​=2⊕2=0=02​ doesn't have its first bit set\nA3⊕A5=4⊕2=6=1102A_3 \\oplus A_5 = 4 \\oplus 2 = 6 = 110_2A3​⊕A5​=4⊕2=6=1102​ has its first bit set\nSo, the answer is 222.\n\n\nQuery 222: the ranges are [1,2][1, 2][1,2] and [3,5][3, 5][3,5], and now k=2k = 2k=2. This time, there are 666 pairs of indices. Of them, it can be verified that (1,3)(1, 3)(1,3) and (2,3)(2, 3)(2,3) are the ones that satisfy the given condition."
